classmethods::
method:: ar
argument:: freq
Cycle frequency in Hz, clipped to 0.001..sampleRate/(numPoints+1), sampled at cycle entry. Each cycle advances one simulation step. Non-finite input uses 220 Hz.
argument:: diffusivity
Physical D, clipped to 0..10, in squared radius units per simulation-time unit. Controls both noise variance and drift; zero freezes radii. Sampled at cycle entry; non-finite input uses 1.
argument:: dimensions
Bessel dimension d, clipped to 1..16; fractional values are supported. One removes the radial drift while retaining diffusion. Sampled at cycle entry; non-finite input uses 2.
argument:: timeStep
Simulation-time increment per waveform cycle, clipped to 0..0.01; default 0.001. Zero freezes radii. This is independent of the audio sample duration. Sampled at cycle entry; non-finite input uses 0.001.
argument:: modFreq
Rate of the common x-modulation sine in Hz, clipped to 0..sampleRate/2. Phase advances each sample and is read into the polygon once per cycle. Use rates much lower than freq to avoid modulation aliasing; zero at reset disables x modulation. Sampled at cycle entry; non-finite input uses 0.2 Hz.
argument:: numPoints
Construction-time number of particles, clipped to 2..64 and truncated to an integer, excluding the two zero endpoints. The default 36 follows the paper's example. Recreate the Synth to change it.
argument:: reset
Nonpositive-to-positive edge rewinds radii, waveform and modulation phases, and the original private seed. Audio-rate edges are supported; the reset sample equals the first sample of a fresh instance. Holding reset high does not repeatedly restart.
argument:: seed
Construction-time local PCG32 seed. Nonzero seeds repeat for identical inputs and rate. Zero derives a seed from the parent Synth RNG once; reset reuses it. Cross-platform math rounding can differ.
returns::
One audio-rate waveform bounded to -1..1 before external scaling. No control-rate constructor is provided.

examples::
subsection:: Minimal example
code::
(
{ LeakDC.ar(GendyDiffusion.ar(freq: 150, seed: 123)) ! 2 * 0.08 * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05) }.play
)
::

subsection:: First-order DSS and radial diffusion
Left is Gendy1977 with fixed durations; right is the DDSS reconstruction at the same pitch and moving-point count. They have different innovations, mapping and endpoint geometry, so equal seeds do not imply equal paths. The comparison isolates the practical sound of the models, not a controlled equation experiment.
code::
(
{
    var first = Gendy1977.ar(minFreq: 150, maxFreq: 150,
        ampStep: 0.03, durStep: 0, numPoints: 36, seed: 123);
    var diffusion = GendyDiffusion.ar(150, timeStep: 0.001,
        modFreq: 0, numPoints: 36, seed: 123);
    LeakDC.ar([first, diffusion]) * 0.08 * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05)
}.play
)
::

subsection:: Remove the radial drift
Matched seeds give common Gaussian innovations. Left uses dimension 1 (zero drift), right dimension 2. Both still diffuse; both use the same origin regularization and angular mapping. Drift changes the radius history, while amplitude reflection can reverse the apparent direction of waveform motion.
code::
(
{
    var x = GendyDiffusion.ar(150, dimensions: [1, 2], timeStep: 0.001,
        modFreq: 0, seed: 1729);
    LeakDC.ar(x) * 0.08 * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05)
}.play
)
::
